> nw
You move toward the traces of motion, and the dream shifts...

[0m[4m“On your knees!” You drop down, too scared to resist. Two men in gold-fringed
shawls circle around until they’re side by side in front of you. The wiry one
keeps his speartip in your face while the stout one pulls out a tablet.

“Nabuu-ahu-eresh of Babylon, goldsmith of the Temple. For your crimes against
His Majesty, Siin-ahhe-eriba, King of the World, King of Assyria, King of the
Four Quarters, for your treason and your role in the conspiracy to his murder,
you are hereby sentenced to death by impalement.”

Your breath catches in your throat. “T-treason? Conspiracy? I don’t—what is
this—I don’t know what you’re—”

Your protests are cut off by a warning jab of the spear. You try one more time
to force out the words. “I’m innocent! I swear by all the gods! The
conspiracy—that wasn’t—my brother and me, we knew about it, but we made a
report, he went to Nineveh to tell the palace! He can confirm what I’m saying,
we’ll both swear to it in court! Sharhiya of Babylon, he’s a goldsmith too,
he’s—”

The wiry man spits and tosses his spear down in disgust. “Your brother? Oh, he’s
told us [0mplenty[0m[4m.” He grabs the torch from the wall and holds it up, letting you
see the fallen, crumpled shape lying in the shadows. [0mNo...[0m[4m

The stout man looks back at his tablet. “There will be no trial. The sentence is
final, by order of Prince Urad-Mullissi.” He turns and strides off into the
darkness, while the wiry one looks down at you with a sneer. “Any final words?”
[0m
