id: 1778640301000
parent-id: 1778640300998
command: n
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
> n
You move toward the traces of motion, and the dream shifts...

[0m[4m“And what is your business with His Majesty, ‘Sharhiya of Babylon’?” The wiry
man looks you up and down with obvious distaste, and you try not to flinch. You
wore your best clothes for this, but there’s a vast gap between your best
clothes and royal gold.

“I have important information for the King. About a threat to his royal
personage. I sent a letter—”

The stout man flips his tablet over and clears his throat. “He’s right. It’s
recorded here. We’re to take him to the throne room immediately.”

The wiry man sighs and gestures toward you. “Fine. But first, your cloak. Give
it here. No Babylonian can look at the unveiled face of royalty.”

You grit your teeth and comply. Another indignity as he takes your cloak, folds
it over, and ties it around your head as a blindfold. Maybe your brother was
right; maybe you never should have come here. But you can hear the grand door
being pushed open, and you’re pulled forward by the arm.

“Sharhiya of Babylon.” The stout man’s voice rings out with surprising power.
“You stand before the Great King, Siin-ahhe-eriba, Mighty King, King of the
World, King of Assyria, King of the Four Quarters...” You tune out the litany
and rehearse your speech, waiting for him to finish. “...your appeal is granted.
Speak with your own voice.”

“Your Majesty.” You lick your lips. They’ve suddenly gone dry. “Your oldest son,
Urad-Mullissi...he’s...he’s not happy about being passed over as crown prince.
He’s planning to murder you and seize the throne.”

The silence stretches on and on. Finally, a new voice speaks, young and strong.
Not the voice you’d expected from an elderly king.

“So the reports were right. And if I hadn’t intervened, you would have told my
father. I should thank the gods that I got to you first.”
[0m
