> e
You move toward the traces of motion, and the dream shifts...

[0m[4m“Hey, what’s—”

You grab your brother’s wrist and pull him down next to you, putting a finger
over your lips. “Shh! Listen.” He doesn’t understand, but he complies. You squat
together behind the massive pithos.

A moment later you see them: two men carrying torches, moving toward the pool at
the center of the room. One hands his torch to the other and kneels to rinse his
hands in the water; then his companion does the same.

“...absolutely sure of the report.” That has to be the high priest; you would
recognize his voice anywhere. “Urad-Mullissi might have accepted someone else as
crown prince, but a son of a secondary wife? It’s insult on top of insult.”

“And he’s really going to try...”

The high priest nods. “It’s the only way he could take the throne. But every
vassal has sworn loyalty to Asshur-ahu-iddina. It will mean civil war. Chaos.”

“Then what will we do to prevent it?”

“Prevent it?” The high priest shakes his head. “No. Civil war can only be to our
benefit. As soon as the omens show a clear victor, we offer Marduk’s blessing to
him. All of the Empire will see that Marduk is the kingmaker, the one who
confers true power. And the Temple will return to its old prominence.”

The two figures turn and start to move away, and you can’t make out what they
say next. But the high priest’s last words come in a sharp whisper: “No. Tell no
one, on pain of death.”
