id: 1778703929051
parent-id: 1778703929050
command: ne
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
> ne
You move toward the traces of motion, and the dream shifts...

[0m[4mYou don’t dare take the blindfold off, or run, or anything drastic—so you’re
left frozen there while the people around you discuss your fate. You were
betrayed. Who else could the man on the throne be except Urad-Mullissi?

The sound of your name pierces through your thoughts. “Sharhiya of Babylon, was
it?” (You try your best to nod.) “You can take that off now. The little
charade’s served its purpose.”

As your eyes come into focus, you can see him directly now: tall and broad,
built like a wrestler, with fierce eyes and a thick beard. He waits patiently
for you to fumble your cloak back on.

“Now, Sharhiya of Babylon, this is very important. I need you to tell me exactly
what you heard, and who you heard it from. Tzillaya, please get this verbatim.”
The stout man nods and pulls out a new stylus. Then all three of them look at
you expectantly while you stammer and grasp for the words.

Eventually, Urad-Mullissi sighs and stands up from the throne. “I swear to
Asshur that you will not be punished for anything you speak of today. Nabiya,
Tzillaya, make sure he’s properly compensated, with a little extra for lodgings
until he returns to Babylon. But I need to know every detail, exactly as you
remember it.

“You see,” he steps toward you, and you have to fight the urge to back away, “my
brother has been planning something, he and his mother. He doesn’t know how to
quit while he’s ahead. Father’s gotten suspicious. And now he’s overplayed his
hand—now, I have proof that he’s trying to frame me for treason. This testimony
should be enough to get him exiled, or worse.”

The prince sees your flinch, and stops moving closer. “So I would consider it a
great favor, to me and to my father, if you’d testify under oath. I know the
royal family is...not popular in Babylon, for very good reason. But avoiding a
civil war would be better for us all. And...perhaps when I take the throne, I
can try to mend some of those rifts.”

So you think back, and start to recount your story...
